'''''Anguilla bengalensis labiata''''', the '''African mottled eel,''' is a subspecies of [[eel]] in the genus ''[[Anguilla (fish)|Anguilla]]'' of the family [[Anguillidae]].

Showing the typical characteristics of the Anguillidae, this species grows to 1.75&nbsp;m and as much as 20&nbsp;kg. The adult diet consists of [[crab]]s, [[frog]]s, and [[insect]]s, as well as fish.<ref name="fishbase">{{FishBase species alt |ID = 1273 | taxon = Anguilla bengalensis labiata | year = 2006 | month = 05}}</ref> It is found in east Africa: [[Lake Kariba]], middle [[Zambezi River|Zambezi]], [[Pungwe River|Pungwe]], and [[Buzi River (Mozambique)|Buzi]] systems, Upper and Lower [[Save River (Africa)|Save]]/[[Rhunde River|Rhunde]] system, [[Umzingwani River|Umzingwani]] and [[Limpopo River]]s. The dorsal side of the fish is mottled yellow olive, the ventral surface lighter; adults are less obviously mottled than the juveniles.<ref name="fisher">{{cite web|url=http://www.samara.co.zw/fisherman/species/spec2.html |title=Species Fact File: African Mottled Eel (Anguilla bengalensis labiata) |publisher=The Zimbabwe Fisherman On-line |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060518004411/http://www.samara.co.zw/fisherman/species/spec2.html |archive-date=2006-05-18 }}</ref>

